<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="targets" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Learning Target Quizzes</title>

    <p>
        <alert>
        MTH 124
    </alert>
    </p>

<p>
    Each week we will have a learning target quiz.  Each quiz will ask 
    you content about 1-4 of the target topics below. Each target will
    appear on two successive quizzes, and you can take a third attempt 
    in office hours.  Once you are satisfied with your grade on a target, 
    you never have to take it again!
    </p>
    <p>
        Scoring:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The successful demonstration of Excellence (E) on one of these
            targets gets you 100% on that target.  That amounts to 3% of your semester grade, therefore Excellence on
            all the targets is worth 36% of your final grade in the course.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                     A demonstration of Satisfactory (S) on a target is like scoring a 75% 
        on that target.  
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A Not Yet (NY) on a target is 0%.  Try it again!  (Maybe ask during 
        office hours or at the tutoring center?)
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
  </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>CORE Learning Targets</title>

        <p>
            The following are the 12 Core Learning Targets for this semester.
            
           
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can use function notation, find and simplify the
                        output of a function given an input, and find an input
                        that produces a given output; I can do this for
                        functions given by a formula, a table, and a graph.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can find the average rate of change in a function on a
                        given interval and state the units and interpret the
                        meaning of the average rate of change in applied
                        contexts.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can find an equation for any linear function in
                        point-slope and slope-intercept form, given appropriate
                        data or information.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can find and simplify values of the composition of two
                        functions.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can determine whether a function has an inverse and
                        find values of the inverse given input-output pairs of
                        the original.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can generate an accurate, labeled graph of a circular
                        function given appropriate information about the circle
                        being traversed, and describe key features of the
                        function.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can find the amplitude, period, and midline of a
                        transformed version of the basic sine or cosine
                        function; and I can find the formulas for transformed
                        versions of the basic sine or cosine function that have
                        certain described properties.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Given two points or an appropriate collection of
                        features, I can find a formula for an exponential
                        function that has those characteristics.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can identify the intervals where a function is
                        increasing, decreasing, concave down, or concave up, and
                        I can provide examples of familiar functions that have
                        given increasing / decreasing and concave down / concave
                        up behaviors.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can find exact values for exponential models and exact
                        values of solutions to equations involving exponential
                        and logarithmic expressions in contextual settings.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Given a right triangle with partial information about
                        its sides and angles, I can determine the missing
                        information for the remaining sides and angles, even if
                        the quantities involve an unknown variable.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        I can identify the degree, coefficients, constant and
                        linear terms, and leading coefficient of a polynomial,
                        and identify zeroes, the multiplicity of zeroes, and
                        turning points of a polynomial given its graph.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>
</section>